    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Finset.2Euniv.20fails.20to.20synthesize.20Fintype.20instance/near/579473781\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Typeclass inference will never find <code>all</code> because it's not in the typeclass system and can't be inferred by unification, so that instance will never fire.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Even if it were (via <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Fact#doc\">docs#Fact</a>), it wouldn't find <code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, and <code>C</code> either; hence why you need to put all of them into the typeclass system as I do above</p>",
        "id": 580318845,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1773882843
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Awesome , thanks for linking to <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Fact#doc\">docs#Fact</a> that looks really interesting.</p>\n<p>One more thing, <code>HasThreeElems</code> reminded me of an attempt I tried yesterday</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span>\n<span class=\"n\">deriving</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecidableEq</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"o\">({</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">),</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"n\">cases</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;;&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I was expecting <code>deriving Fintype</code> to work. I think that a \"non-recursive\" inductive type can be made into a <code>Fintype</code> in an \"obvious way\" , so should this be a feature? I haven't read much on deriving handlers so linking to a relevant resource would help me getting up to speed on this.(edit: checking tpil , chapter 10 on typeclasses doesn't talk about <code>deriving</code> , i'll dig into this when i have more time , a nudge in the right direction would still be really useful)</p>",
        "id": 580439678,
        "sender_full_name": "Jad Abou Hawili",
        "timestamp": 1773929363
    },
